{"content_hash": "18acc79a2e5ab1b1", "version_id": 297, "article_id": 30, "level_code": "ja_n4", "lang": "ja", "level": 2, "level_label": "N4 初級", "title": "木の「毛布」で永久凍土を守る カナダの会社がテスト", "title_zh": "用木头“毯子”保护永久冻土，加拿大公司在做实验", "has_translation": true, "topic": "science", "source": "nature", "source_url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03045-8", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.rawpixel.com/image_1300/cHJpdmF0ZS9sci9pbWFnZXMvd2Vic2l0ZS8yMDIyLTA0L2ZsNDg0MTYwMDkzMjYtaW1hZ2Uta3A0dndxanUuanBn.jpg", "credit": "U.S. Department of Energy · cc0 1.0 · via rawpixel"}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 390, "reading_minutes": 1.2, "lead": "北極の永久凍土がとけています。とけると、温室効果ガスが出ます。カナダの会社が、木の毛布でこれを止めようとしています。", "lead_zh": "北极的永久冻土正在融化。一融化，就会释放温室气体。加拿大一家公司想用木头毯子阻止这件事。", "preview": "カナダの会社「パーマC」が新しい方法を試しています。永久凍土の上に、木くずや土、草の種、ヤシの実の繊維で作った毛布のようなものを置きます。すると、土がこわれにくくなります。会社の人は「土の中の炭素を、そのまま地下に残したい」と言っています。", "preview_zh": "加拿大公司“PermaC”正在尝试一种新方法。他们在永久冻土上铺一种像毯子一样的东西，材料是木屑、泥土、草籽和椰子壳纤维。这样土壤就不容易塌坏。公司的人说：“我们想让土里的碳一直留在地下。”", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 287, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@8760f31/content/audio/30/ja_n4.mp3", "duration": 82.688, "size_bytes": 499824, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 281, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@8760f31/content/data/versions/297.json", "paragraphs": ["カナダの会社「パーマC」が新しい方法を試しています。永久凍土の上に、木くずや土、草の種、ヤシの実の繊維で作った毛布のようなものを置きます。すると、土がこわれにくくなります。会社の人は「土の中の炭素を、そのまま地下に残したい」と言っています。", "北極では、人間が出すガスで気温が上がり、こおった土がとけています。とけた所では、CO2やメタンというガスが出ます。このガスの量は、日本が出す量と同じぐらいだと言われています。だから、とけを止めることは大切です。", "会社は今年、カナダの北西地域で初めてテストをしました。毛布を置いた所は、とけたりくずれたりしませんでした。しかし、毛布がない所は5メートル後ろに下がりました。会社の人は「とても良い結果です」と言います。", "むずかしい問題もあります。毛布がどのくらいガスを止めたか、正確に数えるのは大変です。会社は、10年で2万か所に毛布を置きたいと考えています。うまくいけば、ハンガリーと同じぐらいのガスを止められるかもしれません。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["加拿大公司“PermaC”正在尝试一种新方法。他们在永久冻土上铺一种像毯子一样的东西，材料是木屑、泥土、草籽和椰子壳纤维。这样土壤就不容易塌坏。公司的人说：“我们想让土里的碳一直留在地下。”", "在北极，人类排放的气体让气温升高，冻结的土壤正在融化。融化的地方会释放二氧化碳和甲烷。据说这些气体的量跟日本排放的量差不多。所以，阻止融化很重要。", "公司今年在加拿大西北地区做了第一次测试。铺了毯子的地方没有融化或塌陷。但是没铺毯子的地方后退了5米。公司的人说：“结果非常好。”", "不过也有难题。毯子到底减少了多少气体，很难准确计算。公司想在10年里给2万个地方铺上毯子。如果顺利，也许能减少相当于匈牙利那么多的气体。"], "body": "カナダの会社「パーマC」が新しい方法を試しています。永久凍土の上に、木くずや土、草の種、ヤシの実の繊維で作った毛布のようなものを置きます。すると、土がこわれにくくなります。会社の人は「土の中の炭素を、そのまま地下に残したい」と言っています。\n\n北極では、人間が出すガスで気温が上がり、こおった土がとけています。とけた所では、CO2やメタンというガスが出ます。このガスの量は、日本が出す量と同じぐらいだと言われています。だから、とけを止めることは大切です。\n\n会社は今年、カナダの北西地域で初めてテストをしました。毛布を置いた所は、とけたりくずれたりしませんでした。しかし、毛布がない所は5メートル後ろに下がりました。会社の人は「とても良い結果です」と言います。\n\nむずかしい問題もあります。毛布がどのくらいガスを止めたか、正確に数えるのは大変です。会社は、10年で2万か所に毛布を置きたいと考えています。うまくいけば、ハンガリーと同じぐらいのガスを止められるかもしれません。", "body_zh": "加拿大公司“PermaC”正在尝试一种新方法。他们在永久冻土上铺一种像毯子一样的东西，材料是木屑、泥土、草籽和椰子壳纤维。这样土壤就不容易塌坏。公司的人说：“我们想让土里的碳一直留在地下。”\n\n在北极，人类排放的气体让气温升高，冻结的土壤正在融化。融化的地方会释放二氧化碳和甲烷。据说这些气体的量跟日本排放的量差不多。所以，阻止融化很重要。\n\n公司今年在加拿大西北地区做了第一次测试。铺了毯子的地方没有融化或塌陷。但是没铺毯子的地方后退了5米。公司的人说：“结果非常好。”\n\n不过也有难题。毯子到底减少了多少气体，很难准确计算。公司想在10年里给2万个地方铺上毯子。如果顺利，也许能减少相当于匈牙利那么多的气体。", "text": "カナダの会社「パーマC」が新しい方法を試しています。永久凍土の上に、木くずや土、草の種、ヤシの実の繊維で作った毛布のようなものを置きます。すると、土がこわれにくくなります。会社の人は「土の中の炭素を、そのまま地下に残したい」と言っています。 北極では、人間が出すガスで気温が上がり、こおった土がとけています。とけた所では、CO2やメタンというガスが出ます。このガスの量は、日本が出す量と同じぐらいだと言われています。だから、とけを止めることは大切です。 会社は今年、カナダの北西地域で初めてテストをしました。毛布を置いた所は、とけたりくずれたりしませんでした。しかし、毛布がない所は5メートル後ろに下がりました。会社の人は「とても良い結果です」と言います。 むずかしい問題もあります。毛布がどのくらいガスを止めたか、正確に数えるのは大変です。会社は、10年で2万か所に毛布を置きたいと考えています。うまくいけば、ハンガリーと同じぐらいのガスを止められるかもしれません。", "vocab": [{"word": "永久凍土", "pos": "noun", "zh": "永久冻土", "note": "北极常见的冻结土壤"}, {"word": "毛布", "pos": "noun", "zh": "毯子", "note": "这里指像毯子的材料"}, {"word": "会社", "pos": "noun", "zh": "公司", "note": "常用词"}, {"word": "土", "pos": "noun", "zh": "土壤、泥土", "note": "基础词"}, {"word": "とける", "pos": "verb", "zh": "融化", "note": "常与冰、雪搭配"}, {"word": "ガス", "pos": "noun", "zh": "气体", "note": "这里指温室气体"}, {"word": "テスト", "pos": "noun", "zh": "测试、实验", "note": "外来语"}, {"word": "メートル", "pos": "noun", "zh": "米", "note": "长度单位"}, {"word": "正確に", "pos": "adv", "zh": "准确地", "note": "来自な形容词「正確」"}, {"word": "問題", "pos": "noun", "zh": "问题", "note": "常用词"}], "title_original": "Woody ‘blankets’ are being deployed on Arctic permafrost to reduce carbon emissions", "summary_original": "Nature, Published online: 08 October 2026;\ndoi:10.1038/d41586-026-03045-8\nA start-up company is testing whether stabilizing areas of rapidly eroding permafrost can prevent greenhouse-gas emissions.", "body_original": "NEWS\n08 October 2026\nWoody ‘blankets’ are being deployed on Arctic permafrost to reduce carbon emissions\nA start-up company is testing whether stabilizing areas of rapidly eroding permafrost can prevent greenhouse-gas emissions.\nBy\nJames Dinneen\nJames Dinneen\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEmail\nBluesky\nFacebook\nLinkedIn\nReddit\nWhatsapp\nX\nSave article\nView saved research\nA Canadian start-up firm is developing a way to prevent greenhouse-gas emissions from permafrost by stabilizing the soil with an insulating ‘blanket’ of woody materials. If the method works, it could be the first tool to directly forestall green-house gas emissions that are escaping from permafrost as the Arctic heats.\n“Our goal is to make sure the carbon in the soil remains buried,” says Cansu Culha, co-founder of PermaC and a natural-hazards researcher at the University of British Columbia in Vancouver, Canada.\nEarth’s\nfrozen environments are thawing\nas temperatures rise in response to human emissions. The only reliable way to slow that melting on a global scale is to slash emissions from the burning of fossil fuels. But some researchers are exploring methods to prevent the thaw directly, ranging from placing reflective mats on glaciers to pumping water onto sea ice to thicken it.\nResearchers say that PermaC’s approach is the first they know of to focus on preventing permafrost thaw and its\nassociated emissions\n, which are estimated to be equivalent to those of Japan.\nThe company just completed the first test of its insulating blankets in Canada’s Northwest Territories at an area of rapidly eroding permafrost known as a retrogressive thaw slump. These slumps occur when a hill of permafrost thaws and collapses, enabling erosion to take big bites out of the landscape. This exposes deeper layers of soil to warming, forming hotspots of CO\n2\nand methane emissions. One estimate suggests that these areas of rapid erosion could amplify permafrost emissions by as much as 40% compared to models that only account for gradual thaw.\nThe company’s approach is to stabilize the edge of the slumps with a layer of wood shavings, soil and native seeds, and a burlap-like mat of coconut husks. Culha says she got the idea after hearing about Indigenous communities in Alaska that prevent permafrost degradation by planting trees along the edges of slumps. Similar erosion-control blankets are used in temperate areas, but they haven’t been used to insulate or stabilize permafrost until now, she says.\nPermaC has yet to release results from this year'stest. But the blanket seems to have halted erosion: over the Arctic summer, the edge of the slump without the blanket retreated by 5 metres, whereas the blanketed area saw no measurable retreat, Culha says.\n“That’s very promising,” says Christina Schaedel, a permafrost scientist at the Woodwell Climate Research Center in Falmouth, Massachusetts, who has been advising the company. However, the result is from only one slump over one summer, she adds.\nGreen blanket\nOne challenge is calculating how much CO\n2\nthe blankets prevent from being emitted. Measuring the CO\n2\nemissions from slumps is not straightforward and remains an active research question, says Mark Lara, a biologist who studies permafrost landscapes at the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign. “There definitely are slumps that are spewing out carbon,” he says. But there are “lots of minutiae that could dramatically impact the results”, he adds. For instance, the emissions from any given slump depend on its geometry, the amount of carbon in the soil and how much of that carbon ends up as CO\n2\nor methane after erosion.\nSo far, Culha has only rough estimates for the emissions that the blankets might prevent. Extrapolating from research on erosion rates and soil carbon in the Arctic, she thinks that about 2,200 tonnes of CO\n2\na year might be prevented from being emitted at the average carbon-rich slump. If those calculations are accurate and the blankets can be installed at 20,000 slumps across Canada and Alaska — the company’s ambitious goal over the next decade — she estimates that they might prevent about 40 million tonnes of CO\n2\nemissions a year, roughly equivalent to the annual emissions of Hungary.\nCulha says the company is developing a detailed methodology that a third party could use to verify prevented emissions, which would be necessary for the company to sell carbon credits for emissions the blankets prevent. People could purchase those credits to offset emissions elsewhere. The firm aims to issue credits as soon as next year, and has so far raised Can$1.4 million (US$1 million) from investors, says Culha.\nEnjoying our latest content?\nLog in or create an account to continue\nAccess the most recent journalism from Nature's award-winning team\nExplore the latest features & opinion covering groundbreaking research\nAccess through your institution\nor\nSign in or create an account\nContinue with Google\nContinue with ORCiD\ndoi: https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-026-03045-8\nReprints and permissions\nRelated Articles\nEpic blazes threaten Arctic permafrost. Can firefighters save it?\nThis Arctic town wants to make renewable energy work at the top of the world\nIs lightning striking the Arctic more than ever before?\nThe Arctic is burning like never before — and that’s bad news for climate change\nSubjects\nClimate change\nEngineering\nEnvironmental sciences\nLatest on:\nClimate change\nEngineering\nEnvironmental sciences\nAustralia’s bush fires got worse after British colonization — this is why\nNews\n07 OCT 26\nThe Great Barrier Reef requires special attention in the developing super El Niño\nCorrespondence\n06 OCT 26\nNature has rights — it is time the world recognized them\nComment\n02 OCT 26\nA 7-eV bandgap semiconductor based on silicon-doped α-(AlxGa1−x)2O3\nArticle\n07 OCT 26\nA quadruped robot designed to complete a marathon on a single battery charge\nArticle\n23 SEP 26\nAnodic Pd membrane H2 extraction enhances thermochemical dehydrogenation\nArticle\n09 SEP 26\nAustralia’s current wildfire crisis linked to colonial land-use change\nArticle\n07 OCT 26\nNature has rights — it is time the world recognized them\nComment\n02 OCT 26\nAgricultural liming is a carbon sink in the Mississippi River Basin\nArticle\n23 SEP 26\nJobs\nFaculty Positions at the Center for Machine Learning Research (CMLR), Peking University\nCMLR's goal is to advance machine learning-related research across a wide range of disciplines.\nBeijing (CN)\nCenter for Machine Learning Research (CMLR), Peking University\nAssistant or Associate Professor of Cell Biology\nDepartment of Cell Biology at the University of Virginia School of Medicine invites applications for Assistant or Associate Professor of Cell Biology\nCharlottesville, Virginia\nUniversity of Virginia\nRecruitment Announcement for High-Level Talent at the Center for Agricultural Microbiology\nJoin HZAU's global faculty team to advance research with competitive benefits.\nWuhan, Hubei (CN)\nHuazhong Agricultural University (HZAU)\nOpen-Rank Faculty Position in Theoretical Biology\nThe Department of Biological Sciences seeks an outstanding individual for an open-rank faculty position in Theoretical Biology.\nNashville, Tennessee\nVanderbilt University Department of Biological Sciences\nProteomics Specialist\nBuild the science that shapes the future of human health. Application closing date: 08 November 2026 Join a place where ambitious science thrives...\nMil